// Below is the general syntax for GraphXTheatre files

Theatre:SubClass [Name]
Class:SubClass [Name]
{
	StringVariable           "String"
	DataVariable             (Data)/<Name>
	DataVariable:SubVariable (Data)/<Name>:(Data)/<Name>
	ReferenceVariable        [Reference]
}

// Below is an example with lengthy descriptions
//
// The first line will name the Theatre and optionally give it a specific sub-class (currently, Theatres don't have subclasses)
// Class & Theatre names can include whitespace, but that can complicate things and potentially introduce errors depending on how I write the syntax reader, so use whitespace at your own risk
Theatre [HelloWorld_Theatre]

// After the Theatre line, you can create resources and Actors in any order; although, depending on how I've written the parser, it may be faster to write them in order of appearance (e.g: make a Material above the Actor/Mesh that needs it instead of below it)
// Since Material doesn't have a subclass (yet), I don't include a ":SubClass"
// Class names are technically string variables, but use square brackets instead of quotes; the reason for this is that when copying the value of a data variable from one class to another, you write the class' name in angled brackets, so putting the name in quotes felt a bit wrong since they're being used like a reference
Material [Doom_Shiny] // A Material named "Doom_Shiny"
{
	// A reference variable is simply a variable that references something in the actual C++ code; in this example, the variable "Diffuse" is referencing a preprocessor definition called "DOOM_TEXTURE_DIFF" (which you can find in the header file "r_common.hpp")
	Diffuse           [DOOM_TEXTURE_DIFF]
	Specular          [DOOM_TEXTURE_SPEC]
	// A data variable takes numerical data; the syntax interpreter will (I assume) simply convert it using C++ conversion. Further below, you'll see an example of a data variable taking a name-reference instead which is one of the main reasons I created this custom syntax
	SpecularSharpness (256)
	SpecularStrength  (1.0)
}

Material [Doom_Dull]
{
	Diffuse           [DOOM_TEXTURE_DIFF]
	Specular          [DOOM_TEXTURE_SPEC]
	SpecularSharpness (16)
	SpecularStrength  (0.5)
}

Mesh     [Cube]
{
	VAO      [VAO_HANDMADE]
	MeshData [GRAPHX_CUBE]
}

Collider [Test_Actor_1_Collider]
{
	// Reference variables may or may not be either pre-set or dynamic to your codebase. Currently, the idea is to keep it pre-set (i.e: the interpreter knows that "Dynamic" means "JPH::EMotionType::Dynamic"), but I do like the thought of making this file-format (and/or the interpreter) able to be tweaked by the user to add/change definitions or make it fully dynamic to a codebase (which would be very hard to do)
	MotionType  [Dynamic]
	ObjectLayer [Moving]
	Activation  [Activate]
	Shape       [Box]
	// This is a data variable that is referencing another class instead of taking a numerical value. By writing the name of a class in angled brackets, the interpreter will copy the value of the same variable from the class in brackets to the class that called it
	// In this example, Test_Actor_1's Scale, Position, and Quaternion values are copied into Test_Actor_1_Collider's Scale, Position, and Quaternion variables (the Quaternion variable isn't directly used in this example, but it does exist and is calculated from "RotationDegrees" if not directly set)
	Scale       <Test_Actor_1>
	Position    <Test_Actor_1>
	Quaternion  <Test_Actor_1>
}

Collider [Floor_Collider]
{
	MotionType  [Static]
	ObjectLayer [NonMoving]
	Activation  [DontActivate]
	Shape       [Box]
	Scale       <Floor>
	Position    <Floor>
	Quaternion  <Floor>
}

// If you're not using a sub-class, you can just use the parent class; in this example it would be "Actor" instead of "Actor:RigidBodyActor"
Actor:RigidBodyActor [Test_Actor_1]
{
	// This is an example of setting/overriding a variable inside of a referenced variable. To do this, add a colon to the variable, followed by the name of the variable you want to override. Then, make sure you do the same with the values
	// In this example, I set Test_Actor_1's Mesh variable to the "Cube" Mesh that I made earlier, but I override Cube's "Material" variable to the "Doom_Shiny" Material. I didn't set Cube's "Material" variable, but if I had, this would have overridden the value. Keep in mind that when referencing classes in variables that take that class, a copy of that class is used. This means that I can override Cube's "Material" variable here without it affecting other classes
	// e.g: Mesh <Cube> sets the class' "Mesh" variable to a copy of the "Cube" Mesh, and Mesh:Material <Cube>:<Doom_Shiny> sets the class' "Mesh" variable to a copy of the "Cube" Mesh and changes that copy's "Material" variable to be the "Doom_Shiny" Material
	Mesh:Material   <Cube>:<Doom_Shiny>
	Position        (0.0, 2.5, -6.0)
	RotationDegrees (0.0, -90.0, 0.0)
	Scale           (1.2, 2.0, 1.0)
}
Actor:RigidBodyActor [Floor]
{
	Mesh:Material  <Cube>:<Doom_Dull>
	Scale          (20.0, 1.0, 20.0)
}
Actor:LightDirectional [Sun]
{
	Direction (-0.2, -1.0, -0.3)
	Strength  (0.05)
}

// All variables have default values that will be used if you don't set them. For instance, LightDirectional has a "LightColor" variable that defaults to "(1.0, 1.0, 1.0)" which is the color white in RGB. I want a white colored sun, so I just don't set this variable
// Keep in mind that I could still have set "LightColor" to "(1.0, 1.0, 1.0)" with no problems, even though that's redundant.